{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>If it be torn in pieces.</b> By a predatory beast." "<b>He shall bring it for witness.</b> He shall bring witnesses that it was torn under duress — and he is exempt." "<b>The torn thing he shall not make good.</b> It does not say טְרֵפָה לֹא יְשַׁלֵּם, but הַטְּרֵפָה — there is a טְרֵפָה for which he pays, and there is a טְרֵפָה for which he does not pay. For the tearing of a cat, a fox, and a mongoose he pays; for the tearing of a wolf, a lion, a bear, and a snake he does not pay. And who would reason this out by analogy? As it is written ״וּמֵת אוֹ נִשְׁבַּר אוֹ נִשְׁבָּה״ — just as מִיתָה is one he cannot save, so too שֶׁבֶר and שִׁבְיָה are ones he cannot save (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:53.405561485Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}